# Bind only the synthetic fixture statement to its supplied root lock

The first complete ordinary library gate retains six Contents refusals in the
same fixture material constructor. Its synthetic signed statement copies a
historical hosted receipt, then changes the binary and build-log digests, but
not the root-lock digest. It supplies current include_bytes(Cargo.lock), which
has three added supervisor dependency edges and thirty extra bytes. The archived
hosted lock digest and current bytes disagree. Neither lock nor hosted receipt
is to be changed.

The separate fixture correction assigns statement.root_lock from exactly the
current included bytes before signing with the existing synthetic key. The
fixture already uses /bin/cat and a synthetic build log: this is a consistency
test, never historical hosted provenance, production key custody or admission.
All six old assertions remain unchanged, as do production signature, filesystem,
hash, ownership, permissions and revalidation checks.

Add an ordinary control under the same IO-binding test module. Require positive
fixture material and matching signed/file digests, then independently corrupt
the supplied lock or re-sign a receipt asserting a different lock. In the latter
case update the synthetic state provenance hash and explicitly pass signature/
identity verification before requiring filesystem Contents refusal. In the
former case also require sticky refusal of the originally pinned material.
Mutate only files under that fixture's verified temporary root.

Keep this correction separate from the FD isolation hypothesis. Exact current
source views must permit only its one field assignment and its included new
control, retaining the entire old body for historical assertions. Check GNU and
MSRV, strict lint, all old IO-binding cases and source/mutation contracts before
commit. Preserve the first result and any new compile/oracle refusal. No clocks,
services, server, production enrollment, old evidence or original windows change.

The first v5 builds execute no tests. MSRV reports E0308: the new negative
control passed a SignedBuild to the unchanged verifier, which accepts receipt
bytes. GNU 1.94 instead panics in its diagnostic renderer while reporting the
argument error. Both first refusals and exact source bytes are retained. Pass
the already canonical serialized bytes; do not change the verifier or weaken
the negative oracle. Use a distinct v6 candidate and preserve the v5 lane.

V6 executes all seven cases and is rejected by the unchanged verifier on both
compilers: changing the signed root digest correctly returns Invalid because
ROOT_LOCK_SHA256 independently pins the historical source lock. Preserve all
seven first failures. Withdraw the field-rebinding candidate; it must not weaken
that pin. The corrected v7 fixture supplies the exact 175300-byte historical
lock already retained in the protected bundle and independently identical to
`62114be0:Cargo.lock`, SHA256 be46eaf8...3d62b19. The receipt's original root field
is unchanged. An immutable content-addressed copy is fixture input, not enrollment.

The revised negative control distinguishes wrong supplied bytes (Contents and
sticky pinned-material refusal) from a freshly valid synthetic signature over
a different root digest (Invalid at the independent pin). Cryptographic signature
validity is checked directly; the policy must still refuse. The six original
assertions and every production verifier/pin remain unchanged. This supersedes
only the rejected candidate's new expectations, never a prior result or threshold.
